dart_csp — historical licensing notice
======================================

This file is retained as a historical record. The current state of
the repository is unambiguous: see `LICENSE` (MIT) for the actual
grant.

## Background

The solver core in `lib/src/solver.dart` was originally a Dart port
of `csp.js` from `https://github.com/PrajitR/jusCSP`. The upstream
`jusCSP` repository carries no LICENSE file, which under default
copyright means all rights reserved by the original author, Prajit
Ramachandran.

The maintainer of this repository contacted the upstream author in
good faith requesting an explicit permissive license for the
derivative work, and received no response.

To resolve the ambiguity, the derivative parts of `lib/src/solver.dart`
were rewritten from scratch in a separate Claude Code session, using
only textbook references (Russell & Norvig, AIMA Ch. 6; Mackworth
1977 AC-3; Minton et al. 1992 Min-Conflicts) and with no read access
to the prior solver implementation or to any `csp.js` source. See
the attestation block at the top of `lib/src/solver.dart`.

After that rewrite, the repository contains no derivative expression
from `jusCSP`/`csp.js`, and the MIT LICENSE in this directory grants
the standard permissive terms over the work as a whole. This
repository (`CrispStrobe/dart_csp`) was started from the post-rewrite
state of the now-archived `CrispStrobe/dartCSP`.

The companion repository `CrispStrobe/jsCSP` remains a public GitHub
fork of `PrajitR/jusCSP`, which is the use case explicitly permitted
by GitHub's Terms of Service §D.4 for forks of unlicensed
repositories.

## Addendum — clean-room scope extended to `example/demo.dart`

A subsequent contamination audit found that three demo functions in
`example/demo.dart` (a US map-coloring demo, an N-queens demo with a
list-of-`[row, col]` domain encoding, and a `getUsaNeighbors`
adjacency map) had been carried over from the predecessor project
without being rewritten when the solver was rewritten. The original
clean-room scope only covered `lib/src/solver.dart`.

To resolve this, those three functions were removed and rewritten
from scratch in a separate Claude Code session, in this fresh
repository, with no read access to the prior `example/demo.dart`,
to upstream `csp.js`, or to any predecessor source. The replacement
demos (a German Bundesländer map-coloring problem, a textbook
integer-column N-queens encoding, and a small `A < B < C` ordering
puzzle shown via both the manual `CspProblem` API and the `Problem`
builder) were derived independently from textbook CSP material and
from publicly cited geographic references. After this rewrite, the
MIT LICENSE in this directory applies unambiguously to
`example/demo.dart` as well as to the solver.
